C++ Runtime Polymorphism – Vehicle, Car and Boat

Description

This C++ program demonstrates runtime polymorphism using virtual functions.

The program has one base class, "Vehicle", and two derived classes:

- "Car"
- "Boat"

Both derived classes provide their own implementation of the "move()" function.

Class Structure

          Vehicle
          /     \
         ↓       ↓
       Car      Boat

1. Vehicle Class

The "Vehicle" class contains a virtual function:

virtual void move() const

The "virtual" keyword allows derived classes to provide their own version of the function.

The base implementation displays:

Vehicle is moving

Virtual Destructor

virtual ~Vehicle() = default;

The virtual destructor ensures proper destruction when a derived object is handled through a base-class pointer.

2. Car Class

The "Car" class inherits from "Vehicle" and overrides the "move()" function:

void move() const override

It displays:

Car moves on roads

The "override" keyword indicates that this function overrides the virtual function from the base class.

3. Boat Class

The "Boat" class also inherits from "Vehicle" and overrides "move()".

It displays:

Boat moves on water

Main Function

Two objects are created:

Car car;
Boat boat;

Then their respective "move()" functions are called:

car.move();
boat.move();

Each object calls its own implementation of "move()".

Output

Car moves on roads
Boat moves on water

OOP Concept Used

Runtime Polymorphism / Function Overriding

Vehicle::move()
      ↓
 ┌────┴────┐
Car::move()  Boat::move()

The "virtual" and "override" keywords are used to implement polymorphic behavior.
